{
  "$comment": "The one table of audit event categories and friendly names (Round 14 SPEC section 4.5.5). Read by backend/app/audit/events.py and, through a contract test that reads this same file, by webui/src/soc/components/audit/events.ts, so the two cannot drift. A row that carries the structured 'action' (new rows) is categorised by that action: an entry in 'actions' wins, then its prefix in 'action_prefixes', otherwise System with its token shown. A row without 'action' (legacy rows, and new rows whose writer sets none) is categorised by the pair (action_type, surface): a specific surface wins over '*'; an unknown action_type is System with its token shown, never hidden. Labels are Console-authored copy: plain, sentence case, no em dashes. The (status, storage), (status, rules) and (status, analyst_policy) keys extend the SPEC table, which named only (status, settings), so stored configuration writes are not shown as case work. In the same way, the (user_mgmt, baseline|batch|campaigns|models|chat) keys name configuration writes that older builds stored as user_mgmt, so they are not shown as user changes; (user_mgmt, rbac|settings|setup|users) stay Access and identity through the '*' entry. The (decision, demo) key and the (context, rag|rag_document_delete|rag_precedent_bootstrap|rag_precedent_exclusion|rag_precedent_repair|dashboards|chat_history) keys name changes a person makes (Demo Mode, knowledge and precedent maintenance, dashboards, chat conversations) that older builds stored under an agent action type, so they are shown in People and settings rather than as an agent step. (context, chat) has no entry of its own: the investigator writes that key for cases started from chat, so a person's Stop row stored under it before Round 14 still reads as investigation context. user.preferences_updated is an exact entry in Configuration and data so a person's own preferences never read as a user change. The 'actors' lists derive actor_type for legacy rows that carry none (marked derived); 'actor_labels' gives a friendly name to legacy actor tokens where a list shows them, while the raw record keeps the stored token. Outcomes are not in this table, but the page and the read API share one rule: a legacy row without an outcome reads Denied for access_denied and Failed for error, and every other row without one reads Not recorded, as does a row that recorded 'unknown'; the outcome=unknown filter returns exactly the rows labelled Not recorded.",
